course: everyday
lesson: 95
title: A new baby, looking after siblings and living with parents
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A new baby changes a family: babies cry because crying is the only way they can say they are hungry, tired or uncomfortable, their necks are not strong yet, and they need a lot of sleep and care.
explain: Older children are still just as loved and important, and asking for one-to-one time with a parent is a good idea when you feel left out.
explain: Parents are people too: they argue sometimes, get ill, sad or very tired, and work long hours; children never cause grown-ups' arguments, and they can help a little, but they should not have to look after a parent alone.
explain: Every child has the right to be safe at home; if a parent hurts you or you feel unsafe, a teacher, school nurse or children's helpline can help.
explain: A girl in Texas wants to hold her baby brother; she sits on the sofa, her mum lays him in her arms, and she keeps one hand under his wobbly head the whole time.
fact: Babies cry to tell us they are hungry, tired or uncomfortable, because they cannot talk.
fact: To hold a baby, sit down, ask an adult first and support the baby's head, because its neck is not strong yet.
fact: Small toys must be kept away from babies, because small things can choke them.
fact: Babies need lots of sleep, so keep quiet near a sleeping baby.
fact: An older child is still just as important after a new baby arrives.
fact: If you are left in charge of a younger sibling for a few minutes, keep them in sight and call the adult if anything goes wrong.
fact: When parents argue, it is never the child's fault.
fact: Children can help a parent who is ill or tired, but should not have to look after them alone.
fact: Children aged six to twelve need about nine to twelve hours of sleep a night.
fact: If a parent hurts you or you feel unsafe at home, tell a teacher, school nurse or a children's helpline.
fact: Doing homework before play makes free time stress-free.
fact: Sharing news about your day keeps you close to a parent who works long hours.
exercise: Your mum goes to the shop for five minutes and leaves you with your little brother. What is your job? | Keep him in sight and call her if anything goes wrong | Act as his parent and make all the rules until she gets back home | Leave him and play in another room | Take him outside to find your mum
exercise: Why does a new baby cry so much? | Crying is the only way a baby can say what it needs | The baby does not like its family | The baby is trying to keep everyone in the house awake all night | Babies cry only when they are badly ill
exercise: Lily wants to hold her new baby sister. What should she do? | Sit down, ask an adult and support the baby's head | Pick her up out of the cot by herself | Hold her standing up so she can walk around | Lift her under the arms and let her head hang
exercise: Why must you support a baby's head? | A baby's neck is not strong enough yet | A baby's head is made of soft rubber inside | It keeps the baby from crying all night long | Babies do not like being held by the body
exercise: Since the baby came, Jack feels left out. What is a good idea? | Ask a parent for one-to-one time and help with the baby if he wants | Behave badly so that everyone notices him | Stay in his room all day so that nobody has to deal with him any more | Tell the baby it should not have come
exercise: The baby is asleep in the living room. What should you do? | Keep quiet and keep small toys away from the baby | Play loud music so the baby gets used to it | Give the baby a small toy to hold in its hand while it is sleeping | Wake the baby up so you can play with it
exercise: Why should small toys be kept away from babies? | Babies put things in their mouths and can choke | Babies always break their toys straight away by throwing them | Small toys are too boring for babies | Babies are allergic to plastic toys
exercise: Your parents are arguing loudly downstairs. What is true? | It is not your fault; go somewhere calm and tell a trusted adult if it frightens you | It is your fault, so you must stop it at once | You should go down and decide who is right | You should hide in your room and never mention it to anyone, not even a teacher
exercise: Your dad is ill and very tired. What is the right way to help? | Be gentle, help a little and tell another adult if you are worried | Look after him all by himself and miss a few days of school to do it | Keep out of his way and say nothing to anyone | Ask him to do everything as usual
exercise: Your mum works long hours. How can you stay close? | Plan special time on days off and tell her about your day | Stop talking to her because she is never there | Stay up very late every single night so that you get to see her | Tell her she has to stop working
exercise: Ben wants to stay up later than his bedtime. What is sensible? | Follow the bedtime and ask calmly if it can change as he grows | Sneak downstairs after he is sent to bed | Play games under the covers with a torch | Refuse to go to bed until he gets his way
exercise: How much sleep do children aged nine or ten need? | About nine to twelve hours a night | About five to six hours every single night | About seven hours, like most adults | As little as they feel like having each night
exercise: A boy is hurt by a parent and feels unsafe at home. What should he do? | Tell a teacher, school nurse or call a children's helpline | Keep it secret so the family is not in trouble | Run away from home by himself one night so that he is safe from it | Wait until he is grown up to tell anyone
exercise: Why is it good to do homework before play? | Free time is more relaxing when jobs are finished | Play is not allowed until the evening | Homework is easier when you are tired | Teachers always check what time you did your homework each day
test: A boy in Florida babysits his sister in the garden while his mum takes a quick call. His friend calls him to the street. What should he do? | Stay and keep his sister in sight | Go for a minute, since she is in the garden | Take his sister to the street with him | Shout to his mum and then run off to the street
test: A baby is crying and has just been fed. What might it be saying? | It is tired or uncomfortable | It is angry with its big brother | It wants to play a video game | It does not love its own family
test: Which is the safest way for a child to hold a newborn? | Sitting down, with an adult nearby, one hand under the head | Standing up and gently swinging it to sleep | Lying on the floor and holding it above you | Carrying it around the house all day to show it lots of interesting things
test: Ava hears her parents shouting and thinks it is because she broke a cup. What is true? | Grown-ups' arguments are never the child's fault | She caused it and must say sorry to both of them | She should break nothing ever again to stop it | She should choose which parent is right
test: Why does a child in a family with a new baby still matter just as much? | Parents' love does not run out when a baby comes | The baby will soon be sent to live elsewhere | Older children do more chores than the baby | Parents love their children only for being useful around the house
test: A girl in Belfast looks after her mum, who is often ill, and is missing school. What should happen? | Another trusted adult should know, so she gets help | She should keep going alone because it is her job | She should stop telling teachers why she is absent | She should leave school to care for her mum full time
test: Which bedtime fits a ten-year-old who must get up at 7 in the morning? | About 9 in the evening | About midnight or a bit later | About 2 o'clock in the morning | Whenever they feel tired
test: Where can a child who feels unsafe at home get help? | A teacher, school nurse or a children's helpline | Only their own family, no one else | Nowhere, because family matters must always stay private | Only the police, and only when they are grown
test: Your toddler cousin is crawling near your Lego. What should you do? | Pick up the small pieces so he cannot put them in his mouth | Let him play with them, since they are toys | Give him a few of the small pieces to hold and keep him busy and quiet | Leave them, because he will not touch them
test: can i hold the baby, ill stand up n walk around w her | Sit down, ask an adult and support her head | Yes, walk around the room with her quickly | Hold her by the arms like a toy | Hold her up high above your head
test: baby crying again, whats wrong w him | He may be hungry, tired or uncomfortable | He is crying to be naughty | Babies cry for no reason at all | He cries because he does not like you or your face
test: mum n dad shouted all night, was it cos of me? | No, parents' arguments are never the child's fault | Yes, children cause most arguments between parents | Yes, if you were noisy that day | Maybe, so be quiet forever
test: watching my lil sis for 5 min while mum's outside, can i go upstairs | Keep her in sight and call mum if anything goes wrong | Yes, she will be totally fine on her own for 5 min | Yes, if she is watching TV | Yes, lock her in the room first
explain: A boy in Texas, jealous of his new baby sister, tried to lift her out of her cot by himself; she nearly fell, and his dad caught her just in time.
explain: A girl in Glasgow gave her baby brother a small piece of hard sweet because he was crying; he started to choke, and her mum had to give firm slaps between his shoulders, called back blows, to clear it.
fact: Babies can choke on small hard foods and objects, so only adults choose what goes in a baby's mouth.
fact: Babies' heads and necks are weak, so children should only hold a baby when sitting down with an adult there.
fact: When people say a woman «is expecting», they mean she is going to have a baby.
test: baby's crying, can i give her a bit of my sweet | No, babies can choke, get an adult | Yes, a small bit of sweet is fine for babies | Yes, if you break it up into tiny bits | Only if she stops crying for a minute
test: wanna pick up my baby bro from the cot to show my mate | No, only hold him sitting down with an adult there | Yes, pick him up under the arms, it's only for a minute | Yes, if you're quick | Carry him round the house
test: mum's always busy with the baby, i feel invisible | Tell a parent how you feel, they still love you | Be naughty on purpose so they have to notice you | Hide the baby's things | Run away for a while
test: heard a baby at a party swallowed a Lego piece n went totally blue, the ambulance came in like 10 seconds | Small things can choke babies, so keep toys away; the 10 seconds part is likely exaggerated | Babies can swallow anything safely because their throats are made for it, so it cannot be true | It is true, so babies should never have any toys | Only Lego is dangerous, other small toys are fine
test: my auntie says if u hold a baby wrong its head literally falls off lol | No, but a baby's neck is weak, so sit down, ask an adult and support its head | It is true, that is why babies wear hats | It is a joke, so you can hold a baby any way | Babies' heads are very strong from birth, so there is no need to support them at all
test: everyone says Kai's parents scream at each other every night n it's cos he's so naughty | Do not spread it; when parents argue it is never the child's fault | It must be true, naughty kids are always the reason why their parents argue | Spread it so Kai learns to behave better | Ask Kai about it in front of the class
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reason for which a baby cries? | A baby cries to communicate hunger, tiredness or discomfort, being unable to talk | A baby cries solely in order to annoy the older members of the household | A baby cries because it dislikes the persons who are caring for it at the time | A baby cries only when it is seriously ill and requires a doctor without any delay at all
exam: Ascertain the number of hours of sleep per night required by children between six and twelve years of age. | About nine to twelve hours | About two to three hours each night | About four to five hours each night | About eighteen to twenty hours
exam: Pursuant to a child being left in charge of a younger sibling for a few minutes, select the conduct consistent with the established facts. | The younger sibling is kept in sight and the adult is called if anything goes wrong | The younger sibling is left alone in another room while the child goes off to play elsewhere | The younger sibling is given small toys and hard sweets to keep them occupied | The younger sibling is taken outside to the road without the knowledge of an adult
exam: In respect of a child who is hurt by a parent or who feels unsafe at home, which course of action is correct? | The child tells a teacher, a school nurse or a children's helpline | The child keeps the matter secret so that the family is not troubled | The child waits until adulthood before telling anyone about it | The child tells only a younger sibling and no adult whatsoever
test: overheard dad: "your mum n me have some news" n now they smiling all the time | Maybe they are expecting a baby or have happy news; wait for them to tell you | It means they are secretly angry with you and are hiding it behind smiles | It means you are moving school and they are trying to make it sound nice | Tell everyone it's a baby
test: Nan whispered "she had him very young, before she was married" bout my cousin | Your cousin's mum had him before she married; that is private family business, so do not spread it | It means your cousin is still a little baby and not really grown up yet at all | It means he is in trouble | Tell your cousin to his face what nan said, so that he knows the truth about it
test: Olly says his big sis "is expecting" n kids are laughing | His sister is going to have a baby; that is her private news, so be kind and do not laugh | It means she is waiting for a bus, so the kids are laughing at nothing at all | It means she did something really wrong and is waiting to be punished for it | It means she is ill
